@page "/field-state"

<PageTitle>Field-state visualizer</PageTitle>
<h1>Field-state visualizer</h1>
<p>Every CSS class and pending indicator the kit renders is a decision made from one small record: <code>FieldState</code>. This page puts it on screen directly, live, for both fields below.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "Handle.cs", "FieldStateVisualizer.razor", "FieldStateVisualizer.razor.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Touched</strong>: the user has interacted with the field, or a draft load decided for it.</li>
            <li><strong>Modified</strong>: the EditContext says the value changed.</li>
            <li><strong>Validating</strong>: a check involving this field is still running. It is scoped to the field you edited (before and after a submit alike) and form-wide for a submit, the check you asked for. A draft load stays false for every field: it answers for the whole model, so the engine's own flag is true for a page-level spinner, but no field is waiting on it.</li>
            <li><strong>Errors</strong>: the field currently carries error-severity messages.</li>
            <li><strong>Warnings</strong>: the field currently carries warning-severity messages.</li>
            <li><strong>Blur</strong> has no column of its own, and no flag either: it is what marks touched on this page, wiring <code>field.MarkTouched()</code> to <code>onblur</code> — the context seam meant for custom inputs. A built-in input marks touched only when a committed value change notifies the engine, in every <code>UpdateOn</code> mode, so without this wiring, tabbing through never touches.</li>
            <li><strong>Form valid</strong>: the readout above Submit, kept live by <code>FormidableOptions.TrackFormValidity</code>: an invisible whole-form check by the submit profile, run once when the form is built and again on every change, that shows no message and no "checking…". Submit stays disabled until the whole form would pass.</li>
            <li>Green means a check has answered that a submit would not fail the field, not merely that you visited it. On this page <code>TrackFormValidity</code> earns less than it sounds, because the live check behind every edit already runs the submit rules; what it adds is the answer <em>before</em> any edit: a pristine form that reports truthfully, and the "confirmed" border on a field you have only tabbed through while nothing on the form has been changed yet. A form that narrows <code>LiveProfile</code>, or a validator the engine cannot take rule by rule, needs it for much more, since there a live check alone never earns green. The docs' page <em>How the engine works</em> has the mechanism.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Tab into Username and out — Touched flips to True, Modified stays False. Nothing was typed, so nothing was committed: the blur alone touches the field, because this page wires <code>field.MarkTouched()</code> to <code>onblur</code>.</li>
            <li>Look at Username's border now — it takes none, and Submit stays disabled with Form valid reading No. The validity check's answer already carries the required-Username failure, and a field the engine knows would fail submit is never styled valid, disclosed message or none.</li>
            <li>Tab through Display name the same way and it DOES confirm: the same answer holds nothing against it. Nothing has been edited yet, so no live check has run for either field: this is the validity check's work alone.</li>
            <li>Type <code>admin</code> — Modified and Validating flip to True, then Errors flips once the taken-check lands. Submit stays disabled: a taken username still fails the validity check.</li>
            <li>Clear Username — the field is engaged now, so "Username is required" appears inline at once, from the live check rather than from any submit.</li>
            <li>Type <code>ada</code> instead: once the async check clears, the message goes, Form valid flips to Yes and Submit enables itself with no click needed. Tab out and the border confirms too — a focused box wears the accent border whatever its verdict, so that one waits for the blur. What it confirms is that a check has answered that a submit would not fail the field, whichever of the validity check and the live check got there first.</li>
            <li>Submit — both rows' Validating flip together: submit is a form-wide check, not a per-field one.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<FormidableForm @ref="_form" Model="_handle" Options="_options" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid">
    <div class="summary-slot">
        <FormidableSummary />
    </div>

    <FormidableField For="() => _handle.Username" Context="field">
        <div class="field">
            <label>Username <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_handle.Username" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" @onblur="() => field.MarkTouched()" /></label>
            <em role="status">@(field.State.IsValidating ? "checking…" : null)</em>
        </div>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _handle.Username" />
    </FormidableField>

    <FormidableField For="() => _handle.DisplayName" Context="field">
        <div class="field">
            <label>Display name <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_handle.DisplayName" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" @onblur="() => field.MarkTouched()" /></label>
            <em role="status">@(field.State.IsValidating ? "checking…" : null)</em>
        </div>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _handle.DisplayName" />
    </FormidableField>

    <table class="state-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Field</th>
                <th>Touched</th>
                <th>Modified</th>
                <th>Validating</th>
                <th>Errors</th>
                <th>Warnings</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <FormidableField For="() => _handle.Username" Context="field">
                <tr>
                    <td>Username</td>
                    <td>@field.State.IsTouched</td>
                    <td>@field.State.IsModified</td>
                    <td>@field.State.IsValidating</td>
                    <td>@field.State.HasErrors</td>
                    <td>@field.State.HasWarnings</td>
                </tr>
            </FormidableField>
            <FormidableField For="() => _handle.DisplayName" Context="field">
                <tr>
                    <td>Display name</td>
                    <td>@field.State.IsTouched</td>
                    <td>@field.State.IsModified</td>
                    <td>@field.State.IsValidating</td>
                    <td>@field.State.HasErrors</td>
                    <td>@field.State.HasWarnings</td>
                </tr>
            </FormidableField>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>Form valid: <strong>@(_form?.Engine?.IsFormValid == true ? "Yes" : "No")</strong></p>

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="submit" disabled="@(_form?.Engine?.IsFormValid != true)">Submit</button>
    </div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>

<p><small>The table rows above are not a read-only view bolted on the side — each is its own <code>FormidableField</code>, registered with the form exactly like the inputs above it. That is why they re-render the instant a flag changes: render-registration means any markup a field renders, table row included, is the live proof of that field's state.</small></p>
